# Quest_Design — Skeleton

## 1. Applies when
RPG or adventure genres with formal quest structures (open_world_rpg).

## 2. This document owns
- Quest structure patterns: objectives, steps, gating conditions, branching rules, failure handling.
- Quest categorization (main / side / faction / repeatable) and inter-quest dependencies.
- Quest state machines (offered / active / complete / failed / expired).

## 3. This document does NOT own
- Quest narrative content and dialogue — Narrative_Script / Dialogue_Design.
- Quest rewards as economy values — Resource / Item.
- Where quests physically happen — World / Level.

## 4. Recommended chapter structure
1. Quest philosophy / 2. Categories / 3. Structure patterns / 4. Branching & gating / 5. State machine / 6. Dependencies graph / 7. Failure & recovery / 8. Open questions

## 5. Common boundaries
- Quest vs Mission: Mission owns delivered task *content*; Quest owns the structural system.
- Quest vs Dialogue: dialogue conditions reference quest states by ID, never redefine them.

## 6. Common audit rules
- Every referenced quest state exists in the state machine.
- No quest branch without a defined resolution path (complete/fail).
